@use-modules[(math-derivations commands) (grove math pipeline)
             ((grove math semantic) #:prefix ◆:)
             ((grove math notation) #:prefix ◇:)]
@title{Scalar calculus with explicit domains}
@summary{Chain and quotient rules, reusable declarations and checked real-domain premises.}

@(define x (◆:coordinate 'x))
@(define a (◆:parameter 'a))
@(define f (◆:function 'f #:inputs '(scalar) #:result 'scalar))

The bindings describe an independent coordinate, a constant parameter and a unary scalar function. Their declarations become assumptions only inside an explicit scope. A function reference is distinct from a scalar variable.

@with-assumptions[(list x a f "positive(x); smooth(f)")]{
@heading{Elementary compositions}
@differentiate[x]{@◆:sin{x^2}}
@differentiate[x]{@◆:exp{a*x}}
@differentiate[x]{@◆:log{x}}
@differentiate[x]{cos(x^3)}

The first pass applies differentiation rules, and the second simplifies the generated terms. The final expressions are 2*x*cos(x^2), a*exp(a*x), 1/x and -3*x^2*sin(x^3). Each recorded step includes its rule and source locations.

@heading{Quotients}
@differentiate[x]{x/x^2}

Positivity of x proves it is nonzero and discharges the denominator condition for x^2. The result need not be a canonical rational expression: cancellation and general rational normalization are separate future work.

@heading{A declared function}
@differentiate[x]{@◆:call[f]{x^2}}
@math{@◆:prime[f]{x}}

The result uses f′(x^2). Grove knows the function signature and the asserted smoothness, but does not invent an implementation for f.

@heading{Authored steps}
@check-derive[#:view 'full]{
  differentiate(sin(x),x)
  differentiate(x,x)*cos(x)
  1*cos(x)
  cos(x)
}
@check-derive[#:view 'full]{
  differentiate(log(x),x)
  differentiate(x,x)/x
  1/x
}

@heading{Search for a chain-rule step}
@search-derive["differentiate(x,x)*cos(x)" #:max-depth 1]{differentiate(sin(x),x)}
}

@heading{Missing and false premises}
@differentiate[x #:assumptions (list x) #:on-error 'show]{log(x)}
@differentiate[x #:assumptions (list x) #:on-error 'show]{1/x}
@differentiate[x #:assumptions (list x f) #:on-error 'show]{@◆:call[f]{x}}
@differentiate[x #:assumptions (list x) #:on-error 'show]{log(-1)}

These examples report the required positive, nonzero or smoothness premise, or an invalid real logarithm argument. Facts are explicit author assertions; Grove checks their supported structure and obvious contradictions, rather than proving arbitrary hypotheses.

@heading{An incorrect authored derivative}
@check-derive[#:assumptions (list x) #:on-error 'show]{
  differentiate(sin(x),x)
  sin(x)
}

Natural-number powers are supported. General real powers, higher derivatives of unknown functions and integration are outside this stage. @link["polynomial-differentiation"]{Polynomial differentiation} and @link["vector-analysis"]{vector analysis} use the same derivation certificates.
